{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261126T212656Z-f4781515", "ts": "2026-11-26T21:34:01Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.10.1-dev", "branch": "fix/git-quotepath", "build": "f9e60c885bed"}, "body": {"base_commit": "f9e60c885bedcc2f5b633958f5931a00a1bb5dcf", "base_tree": "c87708bd1cd23ef96828748b0145fe6c5f655e33", "head_tree": "c03af39b91f504f27a386c25631ad33d75c77624", "head_commit": "cedfdd2e119a22b4c31a9c6f801e23b884b79bcd", "dispatch_commit": "cedfdd2e119a22b4c31a9c6f801e23b884b79bcd", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/bin/test-reference-verify", "plugin/lib/buildplan_refs.py", "plugin/lib/change_fragments.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/gitstate.py", "plugin/lib/late_requirements.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_compact.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_files.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/record_lint.py", "plugin/lib/risk.py", "tests/test_git_path_names.py", "tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py", "tests/test_record_lint.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-git-quotepath.md", ".prawduct/changes/git-quotepath.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/bin/test-reference-verify", "plugin/lib/buildplan_refs.py", "plugin/lib/change_fragments.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/gitstate.py", "plugin/lib/late_requirements.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_compact.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_files.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/record_lint.py", "plugin/lib/risk.py", "tests/test_git_path_names.py", "tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py", "tests/test_record_lint.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-git-quotepath.md", ".prawduct/changes/git-quotepath.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Some name reads still go through a runner that strips stdout before decoding, so the porcelain reader the plan counts as fixed still misreads a quoted name", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: `evidence.run_git` defaults to `strip=True`, which strips the whole output before anyone splits it. That eats the first line's leading whitespace and the last line's trailing whitespace, and no per-line decoder can recover them. The sharpest member is `critic_consolidate.no_judgeable_wip`, which the plan's site table lists as fixed 'through parse_porcelain_line'. It calls `run_git(..., \"status\", \"--porcelain\", \"--untracked-files=all\")` without `strip=False`, which `run_git`'s own docstring says porcelain needs. When the first line is an unstaged change (` M \"docs/caf\\303\\251.md\"`), the strip turns it into `M \"docs/\u2026\"`, so `line[3:]` drops the opening quote. `unquote_git_path` then returns `docs/caf\\303\\251.md\"`, which ends in `.md\"` and reads as judgeable. That is exactly the case the change fragment says is fixed. The same strip also takes the first character of an unquoted first-line path: ` M .prawduct/project-state.yaml` becomes `prawduct/project-state.yaml`, which is judgeable. Because porcelain sorts by path, `.prawduct/` entries usually come first, so this is common. The result is that `working_tree_interval_base` refuses the merge-base arm when it should take it. No test covers `no_judgeable_wip`: the porcelain fixtures in `tests/test_git_path_names.py` are untracked `??` files, which have no leading status space and so cannot show the defect. The other members lose a name's leading or trailing whitespace at the first or last line: `prawduct-hook _judge_flakes` (ls-files), `native_review._tree_moved`, `late_requirements.scan` (working tree), and `record_lint._resolve_artifact`. HARD_NAMES includes ` leading.md`, and `critic_mode._paths_since_tree` moved off the stripping `_git_text` for this very reason, but these sites did not get the same treatment. Fix: pass `strip=False` at each member. Close the class with a construction rather than a list: extend `tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py` so a name read through `run_git` must pass `strip=False` (or `-z`). Add a test for `no_judgeable_wip` with a committed-then-modified `docs/caf\u00e9.md`, seen red first.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/lib/late_requirements.py", "plugin/lib/record_lint.py", "tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A git read whose output carries path names and is returned by a runner that strips the whole stdout (evidence.run_git with default strip=True, gitstate._git_text) before it is split into names.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py::no_judgeable_wip"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook::_judge_flakes"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/lib/native_review.py::_tree_moved"}, {"id": "m4", "site": "plugin/lib/late_requirements.py::scan"}, {"id": "m5", "site": "plugin/lib/record_lint.py::_resolve_artifact"}], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "The lint does not scan diff-header reads, though the plan's chunk 02 Tests name them and the change fragment says it scans every argument list that prints names", "recommendation": "Instance. `_is_name_read` in `tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py` matches `--name-only`, `--name-status`, `ls-files`, `ls-tree`, `diff-tree`, `grep -l` and `--porcelain`. A plain patch `diff`, such as `record_lint._added_lines`'s `diff --unified=0`, prints names in its `diff --git` headers and never matches. The plan's chunk 02 Tests list '`diff` headers' among what the lint scans, and none of the recorded mid-build decisions drops them. The fragment's claim that the lint 'scans every git argument list that prints names' is therefore false for patch output. Consequence: the next author who parses a patch's headers by hand, instead of through `gitstate.diff_header_path`, passes the lint, and the fail-open class this change closes comes back for that reader. Fix: make the scan recognise a patch-producing `diff`/`show`/`log -p` argument list (one without `--quiet`/`--exit-code`/`--name-*`/`-z`). Require such a function to call `diff_header_path` or be listed, and add a raw patch-reader spelling to `test_the_scan_sees_each_spelling` as its control.", "files": ["tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py"], "scope": "instance", "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "diff_header_path's mixed-quoting branches have no test", "recommendation": "Instance. `gitstate.diff_header_path` has four arms: both sides quoted, a quoted old side with an unquoted new side, an unquoted old side with a quoted new side (`rest.endswith('\"') and ' \"b/' in rest`), and unquoted. Tests cover only the both-quoted, unquoted and malformed cases (`tests/test_record_lint.py`, `test_record_lint_added_lines_names_a_quoted_file`). git quotes each side on its own, so the two mixed arms are reachable whenever `_added_lines` sees a rename between a quoted and a plain record name. That can happen because rename detection is on by default and both paths can be in `records`. By reading, both arms decode correctly today. Unpinned, a regression in either one silently drops or misattributes a renamed record's added lines, and nothing turns red. Fix: add two `_parse_diff` text cases, `diff --git \"a/caf\\303\\251.md\" b/plain.md` and `diff --git a/plain.md \"b/caf\\303\\251.md\"`, each asserting its lines land under the new name, seen red by breaking each arm.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gitstate.py", "tests/test_record_lint.py"], "scope": "instance", "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The quoted-name lint judges the function that spells git's arguments, not the code that reads the output, so it misses diff-header reads", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: `tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py` decides membership from argument spellings (`_is_name_read`) and credits decoding to any decoder name that appears anywhere in the same top-level function (`_calls_a_decoder`). The property it exists to enforce is that every name git prints reaches a decoder. Three members are open today. (m1) A whole-diff read whose headers carry names is not counted as a name read at all: `record_lint._added_lines` runs `diff --unified=0` with no name flag. That is the exact shape the old `_DIFF_HEADER_RE` reader had, and the plan's Chunk 02 Tests list `diff` headers as in scope for the lint. A new header parser built on a regex would pass green. (m2) One decoded read credits every other read in the same function. Adding a second raw `--name-only` read to a function that already calls `git_path_lines` keeps the lint green. (m3) `gitstate.git_status_output` is exempt by listing, and the lint never examines its readers (gates, prawduct-hook, gitstate), so the listed reason ('its readers ... parse with parse_porcelain_line') is never checked. Consequence: a future reader in any of these three shapes brings back the fail-open this change closes, and the lint the plan names as its guard stays green. Closure: add whole-diff reads (`diff`/`show`/`log -p` with no `--name-only`/`--name-status`/`--quiet`/`--exit-code`/`--stat`) to `_is_name_read`, and list or route `_added_lines`. Write the function-granularity bound into the lint's docstring as a stated limit. Today it says 'A function that calls one decodes what it reads', which is not true of a function holding two reads. Leave the dataflow question to judgment, not to more list entries.", "files": ["tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py", "plugin/lib/record_lint.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A git read whose stdout carries path names and reaches a consumer without passing through a gitstate decoder, while the lint reports it as decoded or does not see it.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/record_lint.py::_added_lines (diff --unified=0 headers, not recognised by _is_name_read)"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py::_calls_a_decoder (function-level credit)"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/lib/gitstate.py::git_status_output readers (exempted by listing, never scanned)"}], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Records Pass (It ships)", "severity": "warning", "title": "Change record claims the lint scans every git read that prints names, but it never sees a diff-header read", "recommendation": "Instance. The shipped fragment says `tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py` \"scans every git argument list that prints names\", and the plan's Chunk 02 Tests list `diff` headers among the spellings it must catch. `_is_name_read` only matches `--name-only`, `--name-status`, `ls-files`, `ls-tree`, `diff-tree`, `grep -l` and `--porcelain*`. A patch-producing `git diff` (the `diff --git` headers `record_lint._added_lines` reads with `--unified=0`) carries none of those words, so it never enters the inventory, and the same holds for `show` or `log -p`. Who goes wrong: a maintainer who adds a new header parser trusts the lint to flag a raw read, and it stays green. The record asserts a guard that does not exist for exactly the reader class this branch fixed in `record_lint`. Fix one of two ways. Either teach `_is_name_read` to recognize a patch-output read (a `diff`/`show`/`log` argument list with no name-only or stat-only flag), add it to `test_the_scan_sees_each_spelling`'s control, and let `record_lint._added_lines` pass because it decodes (in its caller `_parse_diff`, so the lint's owner rule has to reach it or the entry gets listed). Or narrow the fragment's sentence to the forms the lint actually detects.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/git-quotepath.md", "tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "the change record's coverage claim for the git-path read lint", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": ".prawduct/changes/git-quotepath.md: 'scans every git argument list that prints names'"}], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "blocking", "title": "record-lint chunk-ref-missing: chunk 01:120", "recommendation": "declared deliverable `skills/caf\u00e9.md` file does not exist", "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-6"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 5, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The decoder test is an enumerated list, not the property test chunk 01 specifies", "recommendation": "Chunk 01's Tests call for a property test: for any name, git's quoting of it decodes back to the name. `test_every_name_git_prints_decodes_to_the_name_on_disk` uses a fixed ten-name HARD_NAMES list, and invalid UTF-8 is a hand-written literal rather than git's own output. The fixed list does cover each escape class. A generated-name round trip (hypothesis, or a seeded byte generator over the escapes in `_C_ESCAPES` plus high bytes) would pin `_read_quoted` against combinations the list does not hold.", "files": ["tests/test_git_path_names.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "late_requirements.scan now sorts by git's quoted text, not by the real name", "recommendation": "`core.quotepath=false` is gone, so `sorted(line for line in out.split(\"\\n\") if line)` sorts the raw lines. A quoted name sorts by its leading `\"` (0x22), ahead of nearly every unquoted name, rather than by its decoded name as it did before. Only the order of the returned list changes. Sorting after decoding would keep the earlier order.", "files": ["plugin/lib/late_requirements.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The lint sees only string literals, and nothing asserts every git argument list is literal", "recommendation": "Rule cited: 'A lint that SKIPS what it can't statically read needs a companion asserting all IS readable, in the same commit'. `_words` collects only `ast.Constant` strings, so a name flag hoisted into a module constant or a helper parameter (`run_git(p, \"diff\", NAME_FLAG)`) is skipped and the read approved. No current site does this (grep, 2026-11-26), which is the case the rule warns about. Its companion would assert that every `git`/`run_git` call in the plugin has literal-only subcommand words, or list the exceptions.", "files": ["tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "learnings_migrate.dirty_legacy_files is left undecoded, and the plan's Decisions still say it is decoded", "recommendation": "The plan's Decisions and chunk 02's Description route `learnings_migrate.dirty_legacy_files`. The code instead lists it in the lint's UNDECODED with 'the pathspec names only the fixed ASCII legacy files'. That reason is true: LEGACY_REL, DETAIL_REL and HISTORY_REL are ASCII, so git never quotes them, and the change fragment carries the reason class. Skipping it is reasoned and does no harm, but the plan's decision sentence still says otherwise.", "files": ["tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "_parse_diff still splits on str.splitlines, against the change's own rule of splitting on git's newline only", "recommendation": "`git_path_lines`, `risk._git_lines`, `plan_status._log_commits` and `worktree_records` moved to `split(\"\\n\")` because `splitlines` also splits on U+2028, form feed and similar characters. `record_lint._parse_diff`, edited in this change, still uses `out.splitlines()`. Its header names are quoted, but its content lines are raw, so an added line holding one of those characters loses its tail, and the comment '(Content lines cannot reach here ...)' only holds under a newline-only split. The effect is minor and needs unusual record text. Switching to `split(\"\\n\")` would keep the rule in one shape.", "files": ["plugin/lib/record_lint.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "learnings_migrate.dirty_legacy_files was left undecoded, reversing a plan decision, and the reason is recorded only in the lint table", "recommendation": "The plan's Decisions say display-only reads including `learnings_migrate.dirty_legacy_files` 'are decoded too', and Chunk 02 routes `learnings_migrate`. The code leaves it raw, and the lint's `UNDECODED` gives the reason: its pathspec names only the fixed ASCII `LEGACY_FILES`, which I checked and which is true. The reasoning is sound. It is absent from the plan's 'Decisions made mid-build', which is the Records Pass's to rate. Noted here so the oracle-side reviewer sees the code-side fact.", "files": ["tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Framework-Specific Checks do not apply", "recommendation": "The diff touches no skill, template or LLM-instruction file. It is all Python readers, bin scripts and tests, so Generality, Instruction Clarity, Cumulative Health and Pipeline Coverage are not engaged. No action.", "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Plan's decision list says learnings_migrate.dirty_legacy_files is decoded; it was left raw, and the mid-build list does not say so", "recommendation": "The plan's Decisions say display-only reads, including `learnings_migrate.dirty_legacy_files`, are decoded too, and Chunk 02's Description routes `learnings_migrate`. The build left that read raw instead, and its reason is recorded only in the lint's `UNDECODED` table: the pathspec names only fixed ASCII legacy files. The reason is sound, and the names it shows are ASCII, so nobody acts differently because of it. That clears no Records Pass bar. But the owner rules on this list at the boundary, and the reversal sits beside the `worktree_records` one, which is recorded there. Adding one mid-build line would make the confirm surface match the code.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-git-quotepath.md"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Docstrings still describe the retired core.quotepath=false mechanism", "recommendation": "`tests/test_record_lint.py::test_a_non_ascii_pathname_is_attributed_to_itself` still says \"Without the flag the header parser misses it\", but the flag came off `_added_lines` in this change, and the decoder is now what attributes the line. Separately, `evidence.tree_diff`'s docstring now cites `tests/test_git_path_names.py` as where quotepath=false is shown to be insufficient, but that file never runs git under quotepath=false. These are wording only: no test or gate reads them, and no edit is owed. If either is touched, the remedy is to delete the stale clause or make it relational, not to reword the history. (Authoring rule: 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees'.)", "files": ["tests/test_record_lint.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The lint credits a whole function once it references any decoder", "recommendation": "`_calls_a_decoder` marks a function decoded if any decoder name appears anywhere in it. So a function holding two name reads, one decoded and one raw, passes. Every current two-read site decodes both reads (checked: `critic_mode._paths_since_tree`, `coverage._coverage_changed_files`, `native_review._tree_moved`, `change_fragments.branch_change_record`), so nothing is wrong today. This is a precision limit of the guard, and it is worth knowing when reading a green run.", "files": ["tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py"], "oid": "O-10"}], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": "git-quotepath", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02"], "chunks_declared": true, "chunks_e