{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261126T214549Z-11aaae84", "ts": "2026-11-26T21:49:23Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.10.1-dev", "branch": "fix/git-quotepath", "build": "cedfdd2e119a+dirty"}, "body": {"base_commit": "cedfdd2e119a22b4c31a9c6f801e23b884b79bcd", "base_tree": "c03af39b91f504f27a386c25631ad33d75c77624", "head_tree": "5d91fdd6e83434f93e75736759d5e59670eb170a", "head_commit": null, "dispatch_commit": "cedfdd2e119a22b4c31a9c6f801e23b884b79bcd", "mode": "verify-resolutions (delta review, prior findings only)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "inner", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "reviewer", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/bin/test-reference-verify", "plugin/lib/buildplan_refs.py", "plugin/lib/change_fragments.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/gitstate.py", "plugin/lib/late_requirements.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_compact.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_files.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/record_lint.py", "plugin/lib/risk.py", "tests/test_git_path_names.py", "tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py", "tests/test_record_lint.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-git-quotepath.md", ".prawduct/changes/git-quotepath.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/gitstate.py", "plugin/lib/late_requirements.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/lib/record_lint.py", "tests/test_git_path_names.py", "tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py", "tests/test_record_lint.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-git-quotepath.md", ".prawduct/changes/git-quotepath.md"], "findings": [], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 0, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The header-parser rule misses the regex shape R-4 m1 named, which is the reader this branch removed", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: `scan` marks a function as a header parser only when one of its string constants *starts with* `diff --git`. The reader this branch removed was a module-level `_DIFF_HEADER_RE = re.compile(r\"^diff --git a/(?:.*) b/(.+)$\")` (d0904f65:plugin/lib/record_lint.py:330), and its constant starts with `^`, so it would never be flagged. The same is true of an anchored regex inside a function. R-4 m1's predicate was 'a new header parser built on a regex would pass green', and it still does. Only the `startswith(\"diff --git \")` spelling is pinned, by `header_parser` in `test_the_scan_sees_each_spelling`. Fix: test `\"diff --git\" in node.value` instead of `startswith`. Today's only other hits are `gitstate.diff_header_path`'s own docstring and prefix, which `test_every_diff_header_parser_decodes_the_header` already expects, and a module-level match lands under `<module>`, which turns that test red. Then add an anchored module-level regex to the control. With that change, the fragment's rule sentence ('Every function that parses `diff --git` headers ...') becomes true and needs no edit. Separately, the `DECODERS` comment 'A function that calls one decodes what it reads' no longer describes the per-read count the test applies. Delete that clause. m2 (per-read counting) and m3 (status-text readers) are fixed.", "about": {"review_id": "rev-20261126T212656Z-f4781515", "fid": "R-4"}, "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "R-2's consequence still holds for a hand-written regex header parser", "recommendation": "Instance. R-2 said that the next author who parses a patch's headers by hand, instead of through `diff_header_path`, passes the lint. The new header-parser rule catches a `startswith(\"diff --git \")` parser but not an anchored `^diff --git` regex, which is the shape the old reader had. The one-line change in the R-4 observation (match `diff --git` anywhere in the constant, plus a regex control) closes both findings.", "about": {"review_id": "rev-20261126T212656Z-f4781515", "fid": "R-2"}, "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The companion test lets through O-3's own example, a call with one literal word and a hoisted flag", "recommendation": "Instance. `test_every_git_call_the_scan_cannot_read_is_accounted_for` flags a runner call only when none of its arguments is a literal. O-3's example, `run_git(p, \"diff\", NAME_FLAG)`, has the literal `diff`, so it counts as readable while the name flag stays hidden. The companion now covers fully dynamic calls, which is real progress. It does not assert that the scan can read every word that decides a name read. Two ways to close it: flag a `Name` argument that resolves to a module-level str constant, or write this bound into the companion's docstring as its stated limit. Either is fine to accept as is.", "about": {"review_id": "rev-20261126T212656Z-f4781515", "fid": "O-3"}, "oid": "O-3"}], "duration_seconds": 900, "scope": "git-quotepath", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02"], "chunks_declared": true, "chunks_evidence": {"01": {"paths": [".prawduct/changes/git-quotepath.md", "plugin/lib/gitstate.py"], "paths_more": 0, "commits": ["9acfb9025482"]}, "02": {"paths": [], "paths_more": 0, "commits": ["cedfdd2e119a"]}}, "base_reviewed": null, "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-git-quotepath.md", ".prawduct/changes/git-quotepath.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01", "02"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-git-quotepath.md", "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnin